To create a Capacitor plugin for integrating the oneWelcome iOS SDK into your React microfrontends, follow these detailed steps. We'll create a separate repository for the plugin and ensure it works with your existing single-spa root and React microfrontends.

1. **Initialize the plugin repository**

First, create a new directory for your plugin and navigate into it:

```bash
mkdir one-welcome-plugin
cd one-welcome-plugin
```

Initialize a new npm package and Capacitor plugin:

```bash
npm init -y
npm init @capacitor/plugin
```

Follow the prompts, and when asked for the plugin ID, use `com.yourcompany.onewelcome`. Replace `yourcompany` with your actual company name.

2. **Set up the plugin structure**

After running `npm init @capacitor/plugin`, you'll have the following structure:

```
one-welcome-plugin/
|-- src/
|   |-- __tests__/
|   |-- index.ts
|   |-- plugin.ts
|-- tsconfig.json
|-- package.json
|-- capacitor.config.ts
```

3. **Implement the plugin**

Open `src/plugin.ts` and replace its content with the following code. This implementation assumes that you have already set up the oneWelcome iOS SDK in your Xcode project and have the necessary certificates and configurations in place.

```typescript
import { PluginListenerHandle } from '@capacitor/core';
import { PluginListener } from '@capacitor/core';
import { OneginiUserClient, UserProfile, Authenticator, AuthenticationDelegate } from 'one-welcome-sdk';

interface OneWelcomePlugin {
  authenticateUser(options: { userProfile: UserProfile; authenticator?: Authenticator }): Promise<void>;
  addListener(eventName: 'authenticationStarted' | 'pinChallenge' | 'biometricChallenge' | 'customAuthChallenge' | 'authenticated' | 'authenticationFailed', listenerFunc: (data: any) => void): Promise<PluginListenerHandle> & PluginListener;
  removeAllListeners(): Promise<void>;
}

export class OneWelcomePluginWeb implements OneWelcomePlugin {
  private userClient: OneginiUserClient;
  private listeners: Map<string, Array<(data: any) => void>>;

  constructor() {
    this.userClient = OneginiUserClient.instance;
    this.listeners = new Map();
  }

  async authenticateUser(options: { userProfile: UserProfile; authenticator?: Authenticator }): Promise<void> {
    this.userClient.authenticateUserWith(profile: options.userProfile, authenticator: options.authenticator, delegate: this);
  }

  addListener(eventName: 'authenticationStarted' | 'pinChallenge' | 'biometricChallenge' | 'customAuthChallenge' | 'authenticated' | 'authenticationFailed', listenerFunc: (data: any) => void): Promise<PluginListenerHandle> & PluginListener {
    if (!this.listeners.has(eventName)) {
      this.listeners.set(eventName, []);
    }
    this.listeners.get(eventName)?.push(listenerFunc);
    return {
      remove: () => {
        this.listeners.get(eventName)?.splice(this.listeners.get(eventName)?.indexOf(listenerFunc), 1);
      },
    };
  }

  removeAllListeners(): Promise<void> {
    this.listeners.clear();
    return Promise.resolve();
  }

  // AuthenticationDelegate methods
  userClient(_: OneginiUserClient, didStartAuthenticationForUser userProfile: UserProfile, authenticator: Authenticator): void {
    this.emit('authenticationStarted', { userProfile, authenticator });
  }

  userClient(_: Oneg

Here's a simple "Hello, World!" example using TypeScript and Chakra UI:

First, make sure you have Chakra UI set up in your project. If not, follow the installation instructions here: https://chakra-ui.com/getting-started

Now, create a new file `HelloWorld.tsx` and add the following code:

```tsx
// HelloWorld.tsx

import React from 'react';
import { Box, Text } from '@chakra-ui/react';

const HelloWorld: React.FC = () => {
  return (
    <Box textAlign="center" p={5}>
      <Text fontSize="xl" fontWeight="bold">
        Hello, World!
      </Text>
    </Box>
  );
};

export default HelloWorld;
```

In this example, we're using the `Box` component from Chakra UI to center the text both vertically and horizontally. The `Text` component is used to display the "Hello, World!" message with some basic styling.

To use this component in your application, import it and include it in your JSX:

```tsx
// App.tsx

import React from 'react';
import HelloWorld from './HelloWorld';

const App: React.FC = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <HelloWorld />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

Now, when you run your application, you should see the "Hello, World!" message displayed in the center of the screen.

Here's how you can create a Fibonacci sequence using matrices in JavaScript. This method uses the fact that the Fibonacci sequence can be represented as a matrix power operation:

\[
\begin{bmatrix} F_{n+1} \\ F_n \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}^n \begin{bmatrix} F_1 \\ F_0 \end{bmatrix}
\]

Here's a function that calculates the nth Fibonacci number using this method:

```javascript
function matrixMultiply(a, b) {
  const result = [
    [0, 0],
    [0, 0]
  ];

  for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
      result[i][j] = a[i][0] * b[0][j] + a[i][1] * b[1][j];
    }
  }

  return result;
}

function matrixPower(matrix, n) {
  let result = [
    [1, 0],
    [0, 1]
  ];

  while (n > 0) {
    if (n % 2 === 1) {
      result = matrixMultiply(result, matrix);
    }
    matrix = matrixMultiply(matrix, matrix);
    n = Math.floor(n / 2);
  }

  return result;
}

function fibonacci(n) {
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  if (n === 1) return 1;

  const matrix = [
    [1, 1],
    [1, 0]
  ];

  const poweredMatrix = matrixPower(matrix, n - 1

In Swift, you can use the `lowercased()` or `uppercased()` method to ignore case when checking for a key in a dictionary. Here's how you can do it:

```swift
let dict: [String: String] = ["Key1": "Value1", "Key2": "Value2", "Key3": "Value3"]

if let value = dict["key1".lowercased()] {
    print(value) // Output: Value1
} else {
    print("Key not found")
}
```

In this example, the key "key1" is converted to lowercase before being used to look up the value in the dictionary. This means that it will match the key "Key1" in the dictionary, and the value "Value1" will be printed.

Here's a simple example of a Solidity contract for a fixed supply ERC721 non-fungible token (NFT) collection. This contract uses OpenZeppelin's ERC721 implementation and adds a fixed supply of tokens.

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

import "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC721/ERC721.sol";
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/utils/Counters.sol";
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol";

contract FixedSupplyERC721 is ERC721, Ownable {
    using Counters for Counters.Counter;
    Counters.Counter private _tokenIds;

    // Fixed supply of tokens
    uint256 public constant TOTAL_SUPPLY = 10000;

    constructor() ERC721("FixedSupplyNFT", "FSNFT") {
        _mint(msg.sender, TOTAL_SUPPLY);
    }

    function mint() public onlyOwner returns (uint256) {
        _tokenIds.increment();

        uint256 newItemId = _tokenIds.current;
        _mint(msg.sender, newItemId);

        return newItemId;
    }

    function burn(uint256 tokenId) public onlyOwner {
        require(_isApprovedOrOwner(_msgSender(), tokenId), "Caller is not owner nor approved");
        _burn(tokenId);
    }
}
```

This contract has the following features:

1. It uses OpenZeppelin's ERC721 and Ownable contracts as a base.
2. It has a fixed supply of 10,000 tokens (you can change this value).
3. The contract owner can mint new tokens using the `mint` function, which will increment the token ID counter and mint a new token to the caller's address.
4. The contract owner can also burn (destroy) tokens using the `burn` function.
5. The contract uses the `onlyOwner` modifier to restrict minting and burning to the contract owner only.

Before deploying this contract, make sure to test it thoroughly and follow best security practices. You can find more information about OpenZeppelin's ERC721 implementation in their documentation: https://docs.openzeppelin.com/contracts/4.x/api/token/erc721

Yes, you can use `await` inside another `await` function, but you need to ensure that the outer function is also `async`. Here's how you can modify your code to achieve this:

```javascript
async function waitForElementClass(className, callback) {
  const e = document.getElementsByClassName(className)[1];
  if (e) {
    const node = e.cloneNode(true);
    callback(null, node); // Pass the node to the callback
  } else {
    throw new Error(`Element with class ${className} not found.`);
  }
}

async function useWaitForElementClass() {
  try {
    const node = await waitForElementClass("rating-stars", async (err, node) => {
      if (err) throw err;
      return node; // Return the node here
    });
    // Now you can use 'node' here
    console.log(node);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
  }
}

useWaitForElementClass();
```

